#!/bin/zsh
## Minecraft, from the couch, on a controller.
##
## This is Steam's launch target: added once by hand through Big Picture's
## "Add a non-Steam game" (see dot-update.d/minecraft). Deliberately not
## scripted - a non-Steam shortcut lives in shortcuts.vdf, and binary VDF
## surgery is the exact thing dot-update.d/cursor tore out and documented
## as not worth owning. Steam keys a shortcut's per-game settings off the
## target path, so moving or renaming this file orphans them.
##
## Steam launches the game and does nothing else to it. The controller is
## the couch-controls mod's job (github.com/fatlard1993/couch-controls),
## which reads the pad directly through SDL - the same SDL the game itself
## now runs on. Steam Input must stay *off* for this shortcut: it claims
## the physical pad and hands the process a synthetic keyboard and mouse,
## which is precisely the pad the mod is looking for and won't find.
##
## The instance is generated by dot-update.d/minecraft, which pins it to
## the same game/loader versions as the house server.

# An array, not `${server:+--server $server}`: zsh does not word-split an
# unquoted expansion the way sh does, so that form hands Prism one argument
# reading "server localhost" and it exits on an unknown option. An empty
# array simply disappears from the command line, which is exactly what
# --menu wants.
#
# The suite server lives on the rented box now (dot-update.d/minecraft keeps
# the one on this machine retired), so the couch joins it the way every other
# player does: by its address. Loopback was right while the server was this
# box, and goes back in the day it comes home.
#
# Read from priv-config rather than written here, because this repository is
# public. No priv-config, no address, and the launch stops at the title screen
# with the server list one click away.
source $HOME/.dotfiles/bin/lib/minecraft

server=$(mcServerAddress)
joinServer=()

[[ -n $server ]] && joinServer=(--server $server)

# Stop at the title screen instead of joining - for singleplayer, or for
# any settings run where a mouse is already in hand.
[[ $1 == --menu ]] && joinServer=()

# Absolute paths for both: prismlauncher is /usr/bin, but gamemoderun is
# /usr/games, which is missing from plenty of launching contexts - the same
# trap startup.d/steam-bigpicture hit with the steam binary.
#
# exec, and no backgrounding: Steam counts the game as running for exactly
# as long as its launched process lives, and Prism stays in the foreground
# for the whole session.
exec /usr/games/gamemoderun /usr/bin/prismlauncher --launch suite $joinServer
